> The more you learn, the more you realize how little you know. Still, the struggle itself is worthwhile. Knowledge is the root of power, after all.

The Blade Itself is Book One of the First Law series by Joe Abercrombie. It's a grimdark fantasy novel that follows the perspectives of three characters in a fantasy world: Logen Ninefingers, a legendary warrior from The North; Glokta, a former war hero turned crippled inquisitor; and Jezal, a young noble. The story follows them as war between the North, the Union, and the southerners, the Gurkish, looms.

The book switches perspectives with each chapter, gradually bringing the characters closer together. Each character is written masterfully, driven by distinct goals and ideals. As their paths collide, the interactions feel natural and compelling. Glokta is a deeply introspective character riddled with guilt and shame but also hatred and fear, constantly asking himself "why" he does all that he does. It's hard not to sympathize with him despite his actions. Logen Ninefingers appears to be a brute, but he has a well-written overarching arc. Beginning as a lonely man desperate to survive, we see him evolve, build relationships, explore new territory, and defy the expectations his backstory might create. When he reverts to his old self, The Bloody Nine, it's a surprising but not unexpected turn of events. Jezal, the last main character, arguably has the most interesting arc. Initially a snobby, self-obsessed young noble craving attention from his family and peers while maintaining a disdain for commoners, Jezal grows throughout the chapters, showing potential for the later entries in the series. The secondary characters are also well-written, with Bayaz and Ferro standing out in particular.

The plot takes a backseat to character development, which may disappoint readers looking for immediate narrative progression. Many chapters set up little in terms of plot but add substance to the characters. On that note, Joe Abercrombie's prose is rich and vivid, immersing the reader in the vast world he has crafted. His characterization is on another level. However, at times, Abercrombie describes things too much rather than simply showing them. Much of the book's description comes from characters reflecting on past events: Glokta on his imprisonment, Logen on his dark past, or Bayaz on his fight with Kanedias. We are constantly reminded of these moments and the motivations it gives the main characters. While these moments add depth to the characters, they lessen the impact because they're presented retrospectively rather than as current action. That said, this can easily be a positive point of the series after finishing the other books.

Overall, The Blade Itself is a fascinating book by Joe Abercrombie. Its rich prose and strong characters make it a fun read, but it does have a somewhat lackluster plot that ultimately feels like a setup for the following books. As far as first entries go, it succeeds in introducing the characters, making you invested in their journeys, and setting up a grander large-scale plot without losing you along the way.

> Blood gets you nothing but more blood. It follows me now, always, like my shadow, and like my shadow, I can never be free of it. I should never be free of it.
